one and  zinc oxide which in my opinion both kind  of suck ever been zones really good at  blocking UVA but it's not photo stable  which means that after you've exposed it  to too much UV light after it's absorbed  too much and turn that into heat it's  going to start breaking down and once  it's broken down it's not going to  absorb UV anymore so your skin's no  longer protected the amount favored been  zoned is really quite low so if it's by  itself in a sunscreen it turns out that  it's mostly broken down after about an  hour or two there are ingredients that  can help stabilize a Vauban Zone and  sunscreens but still it's not ideal so  this is part of the reason why they tell  you to reapply your sunscreen zinc oxide  on the other hand is photo stable but it  doesn't block UVA very well and in  general you can't really get a PPD or  UVA protection factor of more than  around 20 it's also more prone to issues  like clumping on your skin after it's  been there for too long and if it's  clumped up that means it's no  spread out evenly and so sunlight can  get in between those zinc oxide clumps  in other countries like Europe and Japan  and Australia where I am there are more  options for sunscreens that can block  UVA one there are actually sunscreens  that can get PPG values of over 40 so  this is similar to SPF except for  something called persistent pigment  darkening which happens with UVA the  sunscreen ingredients that can help  sunscreens get to these really really  high UVA protection factors are newer  photo stable organic sunscreens in other  words they are so-called chemical  sunscreens some of these are 10s of s  and 10's of M Max oral SX which is  allowed in the u.s. MEC Sorrell excel  and you vinyl a plus unfortunately these  haven't been approved in the US yet but  you can easily get these sunscreens  online my favorite ingredients have been  to nurse or as an M and T no salt s is  actually in my favorite sunscreen for  daily use which is ultra suta khals  ultra UV daily moisturizer SPF 30 even  though it's only SPF 30 the texture is  really really nice and it spreads and  dries really easily so I can apply a lot  of this sunscreen and since sunscreen  protection scales with how much do you  apply I probably end up getting more  protection with this SPF 30 sunscreen  than I would with a thicker greasy SPF  50 sunscreen most sunscreens also clog  my pores which gives me more acne and  therefore more hyperpigmentation but  this one doesn't it also works really  really well under my makeup it doesn't  boil up or anything like that  I don't know what I drew for PIH if I  didn't have vitamin C it does a bunch of  things with pigmentation the first step  in producing melanin pigment in your  body is turning the amino acid tyrosine  into l-dopa which is then changing to  tow pack winner in the diaper quinone  eventually turns into melanin the enzyme  tyrosinase does both of these steps it's  like a tiny factory vitamin c undoes the  second reaction so it slows down pigment  production vitamin C is also a really  really good antioxidant when UVA  interacts with skin if wants reactive  substances with unpaired electrons  called free radicals these free radicals  are super reactive and they're so  reactive that they'll start reacting  willy-nilly with things in your skin so  things like DNA proteins and lipids and  in this process it damages them and this  process which is sometimes called  oxidative stress leads to pigmentation  vitamin C reacts with free radicals and  stabilizes them so they can't react with  and damage other substances this means  that you get less new pigmentation which  means that your skin is able to heal  existing pigmentation and this happens  with the sunscreen as well I'm quite  basic with my vitamin C so my favorite  vitamin C product is just a DIY vitamin  C serum that I make fresh with ascorbic  acid powder water and baking soda and I  have to remake it every week because  firstly the vitamin C isn't stabilized  so starts breaking down quite quickly  and it's also doesn't have a  preservative and while it's acidic  enough that it probably doesn't need a  preservative I try to stay on the safe  side sometimes I get really lazy and I  just mix a pinch of the vitamin C powder  with the moisturizer right before I  applied on my face which works for me  because my skin is quite used to acid  and so it's not that sensitive to things  like that but if your skin isn't quite  as used to acid perceived with massive  caution because again irritating your  skin can cause more pigmentation  I've talked about my love of lactic acid  before in my acid ingredients video it's  an alpha hydroxy acid that's a little  bit larger than your standard glycolic  acid and because it's slightly larger  that means it's going to be a bit  gentler because it's harder for it to  get into the skin quite as quickly it's  also been found to be better at  hydrating and better treating photo  damage in some experiments I like  glycolic acid as well but lactic acid  just happens to work better for me I've  also been trying out mandelic acid  lately as well since these alpha hydroxy  acids are good for things apart from  just hyperpigmentation so they exfoliate  and they prevent acne as well I tend to  use them all the time but I try to use  them more consistently and more  regularly when I'm dealing with post  inflammatory hyperpigmentation my  favorite latching acid products are  probably Kate Somerville's exfoli Kate  and ultra suta khals brightening serum  exfoliate is a really good product for  people like me who are ambitious but  kind of lazy and you sort of forget to  do your alpha hydroxy acids for a bit  it's a must that you use in the shower  for about two minutes and it acts like a  peel it's particularly good because it  comes out as a thick paste so it doesn't  drip around all over the place and so it  stays on your skin in the shower I  actually tend to leave it on for a  little bit more than two minutes because  I slowly increased my exposure to lactic  acid so I can tell that my skin can  handle it  I wouldn't recommend doing this unless  you're quite experienced with acids and  how acids act on your skin and I'd start  off really slowly following the actual  instructions before building to this  sort of exposure the danger with going  too quickly with strong acid products is  something that a lot of people called  damaging the moisture barrier which is  when you use too much assets and you end  up giving yourself kind of like a mild  chemical burn your skin ends are quite  sensitive and stings and whenever you  use actives they end up just irritating  your skin quite a lot and it doesn't  help your skin recover to wait for your  skin to recover you kind of have to  treat it really really gently and baby  at four weeks and so don't go  true hard into assets without a lot of  caution but if you know what you're  doing  exfoliate is really really good it makes  my skin look smooth and glowy like I've  actually been consistently using a a  Shea for like weeks and weeks it's a  little bit pricey though but a little  does go a long way with this product you  can put a really thin film on your face  especially if you're doing it in the  shower try to get a sample of it if you  can I use a little pencil eraser size  amount each time I also really like  ultra circles brightening serum it's  quite pricey but it was a key part of  this ultra suta khals routine that i did  last year and my pigment faded tons and  tons during that process and so I just  don't really want to mess with this  winning formula for my skin at least  until I run out and I have to repurchase  it I've also tried a few other lactic  acid products that are a bit cheaper  tart knockout tingling treatment is one  that I've recently tried and it's been  really really good the ordinary have  some lactic acids I've tried the 10% and  it's theoretically quite good but it  didn't seem to be quite as effective  when I used it although I didn't really  use it as consistently as I wanted and I  didn't really mohnish my skin that  closely so maybe it does work better it  could also just work better for you  because of your skin's individual needs  I've also been trying out mandelic more  lately and it's been really good but  mandelic acid products are just a lot  harder to get so structure structure  strata soft touch HHA is probably the  most successful one it has 10% mandelic  acid and it comes in this really handy  pump bottle and in general if skincare  comes in a pump I'm gonna like it a lot  more because I'm quite lazy so these  three things sunscreen  vitamin C and lactic acid if I use all  of these ingredients quiet consistently  my PIH usually fades dramatically in the  first two weeks or so after the acnes  gone down and it goes down enough that  it's covered with just my usual medium  coverage foundation and I actually don't  need concealer every one skin is  different so you're fading process your  skin's healing processes probably won't  be the same it might heal faster or  slower but this is what works for me and  the evidence does  show that it does work so give it a try  maybe we'll work for you as well thanks  for watching this video if you liked it  please subscribe to my channel you can  also check out my blog you can also  follow me on Facebook and Instagram  where I post lots of beauty tips see you  next time for more beauty science  [Music]  [Music]

